<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> Why did people ask whether photography could be art?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Because photographers cannot choose their subjects.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Because photography was invented before painting.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Because photographs are always blurry.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Because a camera appears simply to record what is in front of it, so where is the artistic form?</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The answer, developed in this unit, is that photographers make deliberate decisions about framing, light, focus, timing, and subject.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The argument against photography as art was that the camera merely records. The lesson answers with the choices the photographer makes.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Pictorialist photographers</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">imitated painting with soft focus, dramatic lighting, and sentimental subjects, hoping to win acceptance for photography as fine art.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">documented social conditions with political intent.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">refused to compose their pictures.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">rejected painting and used only sharp focus.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Stieglitz's Paula is a pictorialist photograph: soft focus, dramatic light, careful composition.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Pictorialists borrowed painting's techniques, such as soft focus and dramatic lighting, to raise photography's status.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> What did the f/64 Group and Edward Weston embrace?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Soft focus and painterly effects.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Avoiding the darkroom entirely.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">What is unique to the camera: sharp focus, precise detail, and great depth of field.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Hand coloring of prints.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The name f/64 refers to the smallest aperture, which keeps everything in focus. Ansel Adams was a founding member.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Straight photographers rejected pictorialism and celebrated the camera's own strengths: sharpness and detail.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> What is the "decisive moment" in documentary photography?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">The longest exposure possible.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">The peak of intensity in a scene, captured at exactly the right instant.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">The moment a photographer decides to retire.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">The moment a photograph is printed.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The best documentary work, like Lange's, captures a peak of meaning in a single instant.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The decisive moment is about timing: the instant when a scene's intensity peaks.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> How does this unit define sentimentality?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">The falsifying of feelings by demanding easy responses.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">A photograph in color.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">A portrait of a child.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">A very sad subject.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Sentimental work manipulates the image to produce an emotional response that the subject matter does not earn.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Sentimentality is not simply sadness. It is faking or forcing feeling by asking for an easy response.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> What does "the modern eye" in photography accept?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Only studio portraits.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Clutter, accident, and unresolved tension: it catches reality instead of composing it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Only soft-focus images.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">Only perfectly symmetrical scenes.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Vivian Maier's candid street photographs are an example: quick, attentive to accidental arrangements of city life.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The modern eye is the opposite of the pictorialist's tidy composition. It accepts what chance puts in the frame.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> Gordon Parks's American Gothic, Washington, D.C. echoes a famous painting. Why does that matter?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">It makes the photograph less serious.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">It means the photograph is a copy.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">It proves the photograph has no content of its own.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Knowing Grant Wood's painting gives the photograph additional meaning: it shows a Black cleaning woman whose citizenship was diminished by segregation.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. This is a direct test of the claim that background knowledge deepens perception.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The photograph depends on a comparison. The reference to Wood's painting is part of what the photograph says.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> Why does Vivian Maier's work challenge the categories pictorialism, straight photography, and documentary?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">She photographed only landscapes.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">She worked only in a studio.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">She photographed street life for decades without exhibiting it, timing and framing candid pictures that fit none of the categories neatly.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">She sold all her work during her lifetime.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Her case raises the question of how intention relates to artistic form.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Maier took over 100,000 pictures of strangers and never showed them. That makes her hard to fit into the lesson's three categories.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> What did Muybridge's high-speed photographs of a galloping horse reveal?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">That photographs are always unreliable.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">At one moment all four hooves are off the ground, something nobody could see with the naked eye.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">That horses never leave the ground.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">That cameras cannot capture motion.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Photography does not only record what we see; it can reveal what we cannot.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Muybridge used very fast shutters to freeze moments too quick to see. The discovery concerned the horse's legs.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> In Daguerre's 1838 photograph of the Boulevard du Temple, why does the busy street look empty?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">The exposure lasted several minutes, so only the one man who stood still, having his boots polished, was recorded.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">The camera could not see people.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Daguerre removed the people by hand.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">The street really was empty that day.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A long exposure records what stays still and loses what moves. It is a vivid reminder that shutter speed decides how time is shown.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Early exposures lasted minutes. Anything that moved too much did not register.</div>
  </div>
</div>

